The current position contains exactly one immediately activatable threat: the three consecutive x pieces already occupying row 3, columns 1-3. The unique empty square that completes a horizontal four is (row 3, column 0). That square is not yet playable, but dropping in column 0 places an x at (row 4, column 0) and thereby makes (row 3, column 0) the new lowest empty cell of the column. Consequently the move creates a direct winning threat that White must answer on the following turn (otherwise x simply recaptures in column 0 and wins).

No other legal drop produces even a single open three-in-a-row, and dropping in column 5 would immediately hand o a winning reply. After the forced exchange in column 0 the same forcing motif can be repeated:

- x occupies the now-playable intersection (row 2, column 2), completing the diagonal (4,0)-(3,1)-(2,2) and threatening (1,3);
- o is compelled to block at (1,3);
- x then occupies (1,2), completing a vertical three and threatening the top of column 2;
- o is compelled to the useless square (0,2).

The net result is that x places three consecutive stones on high-value central squares while o is forced to two high, low-value squares. Only after that sequence does o regain a free turn with which to activate its own distant threat on the opposite wing. The resulting positional advantage (initiative plus superior stone placement) is decisive.

Thus the unique optimal first move is the forcing occupation of column 0.

